devtunnel-service for Debian and Ubuntu
=======================================

This package installs the devtunnel-service command only. Installing,
upgrading or removing it never signs in to devtunnel, changes tunnel access
rules, writes user configuration, or starts services.

The devtunnel CLI
-----------------

Microsoft's devtunnel CLI is not packaged by Debian or Ubuntu and this package
does not download it. Install it as the Unix user that will run the service,
with Microsoft's installer (Linux x64 and arm64; it writes ~/bin/devtunnel):

  curl -sL https://aka.ms/DevTunnelCliInstall | bash

See https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/developer/dev-tunnels/get-started .
The installer always fetches Microsoft's latest CLI. devtunnel-service was
checked against CLI version 1.0.2030+fc9273aa0f (see "devtunnel --version");
newer CLIs whose JSON output changes make startup and renewal fail closed.
Sign in and confirm access to the tunnel with the CLI itself:

  devtunnel user show
  devtunnel show TUNNEL_ID

Deploying an instance
---------------------

As the service user, preview and then install the user units:

  devtunnel-service deploy --name web --tunnel-id TUNNEL_ID --port 3000 --dry-run
  devtunnel-service deploy --name web --tunnel-id TUNNEL_ID --port 3000 --start

The units run /usr/bin/devtunnel-service, so they keep working across package
upgrades. An upgrade does not restart a running host; restart the instance to
use the new version:

  systemctl --user restart devtunnel-web.service

Removing the package
--------------------

Stop and remove each instance before removing the package; otherwise systemd
keeps restarting a host whose command no longer exists:

  systemctl --user disable --now devtunnel-web.service devtunnel-web-renew.timer
  rm ~/.config/systemd/user/devtunnel-web.service \
     ~/.config/systemd/user/devtunnel-web-renew.service \
     ~/.config/systemd/user/devtunnel-web-renew.timer
  systemctl --user daemon-reload

Removing or purging the package leaves ~/.config/devtunnel-service, the
devtunnel CLI and its login untouched. Delete the instance configuration
yourself if it is no longer needed.
